CENTRAL THEORETICAL POSITION  
    Institutional integrity depends on maintaining continuity between five essential elements:  
    Lawful authority.  
    Entrusted responsibility.  
    Procedural execution.  
    Evidentiary integrity.  
    Independent accountability.  
    A breakdown in any of these relationships can create institutional uncertainty.  
    Where such uncertainty persists, an institution may experience difficulty identifying who authorized a decision, what evidence supported it, which safeguards applied, and who remains responsible for correcting an error.  
    the gradual divergence between:  
    What an institution is authorized to do.  
    What its procedures require.  
    What its personnel actually implement.  
    What its records demonstrate.  
    What its final decisions claim to establish.  
    Authority must remain connected to responsibility; responsibility must remain connected to evidence; evidence must remain open to independent examination; and institutional conclusions must remain correctable.
    
