GOVERNANCE PRINCIPLES  
    The proposed +1HP framework is governed by the following principles:  
    I. Authority Integrity  
    Every material decision must identify its applicable authority.  
    II. Responsibility Continuity  
    Delegation must not eliminate traceable accountability.  
    III. Evidentiary Integrity  
    Evidence must remain authentic, attributable, and independently examinable.  
    IV. Scientific Independence  
    Scientific conclusions must remain subject to methodological scrutiny.  
    V. Professional Competence  
    Clinical and technical classifications must remain within appropriate professional authority.  
    VI. Procedural Integrity  
    Applicable safeguards must be documented and respected.  
    VII. Corrective Accountability  
    Demonstrated errors must have an identifiable correction pathway.  
    VIII. Epistemic Fault Tolerance  
    Contradictory evidence must remain available for examination.
    
FORMAL CONSOLIDATED THESIS  
    Institutional integrity requires continuous correspondence between lawful authority, entrusted responsibility, evidentiary provenance, scientific independence, and procedural accountability.  
    Systemic Obstruction describes the potential fragmentation of jurisdiction, records, authority, and responsibility across institutional boundaries. Such fragmentation must not prevent the identification of the original obligation or the examination of decisions made under delegated authority.  
    Systematic Failure describes the breakdown or displacement of safeguards when scientific findings, public-safety observations, administrative decisions, procurement records, or domestic proceedings are processed without adequate examination. Administrative closure must not be mistaken for factual resolution, and institutional designation must not be mistaken for scientific validity.  
    Schism Fault Tolerance establishes the required institutional failure **metric** for thesis. 
